digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56921-8
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) If understanding and predicting phase transformations is one of the crucial tasks of condensed matter physics and materials science, then studying irradiation-induced phase transformations is essential."];
  "N10" [label="(5,0,0) Currently there is MAX phases show similar multi-stage phase transformation behavior in response to irradiation."];
  "N12" [label="(6,0,0) Currently there is MAX phases are ternary layered carbides/nitrides known as metallic ceramics proposed as candidate materials for nuclear fuel cladding and joint materials due to unique properties."];
  "N14" [label="(7,0,0) Currently there is MAX phases exhibit a unique multiphase transformation from initial hex-phase to γ-phase then to fcc-phase under irradiation."];
  "N16" [label="(8,0,0) Case: Almost all analyses to date were based on the initial phase/structure and the role of intermediate phases has been rarely studied."];
  "N17" [label="(9,0,0) Case: It is a challenge to understand the mechanisms by only analyzing the initial phases."];
  "N19" [label="(10,0,0) Currently there is antisite defect formation energy in initial phases considered as criterion but restricted to early stages prior to γ-phase formation."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently there is irradiation inducing lattice atom displacement and providing the driving force for overcoming the energy barrier between stable and metastable phases."];
  "N22" [label="(11,0,0) Case: Previous irradiation experiments on MAX phases (over 340 types) were mostly ex situ, failing to provide direct observation of evolving atomic structure and complete multi-stage pathway."];
  "N23" [label="(12,0,0) Case: Prior analyses mainly focused on initial hexagonal phases and provided only phenomenological description of irradiation-induced phase behaviors."];
  "N25" [label="(13,0,0) Currently there is no substantial theoretical investigation of compositional trends in the dynamics of these multi-stage phase transformations."];
  "N28" [label="(15,0,0) Currently there is an investigation of distinct phase transformation behaviors of three representative MAX phases M2AlC (M=Cr,V,Nb) under in situ ion irradiation with real-time TEM observation."];
  "N3" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that irradiation induces lattice atom displacement providing driving force and understanding/predicting phase transformations is crucial, therefore studying irradiation-induced phase transformations is essential. (used as induction-common) Common: Irradiation studies are essential for elucidating phase mechanisms in materials science."];
  "N31" [label="(17,0,0) Phenomenon: Cr2AlC follows hex-γ-amorphous transition pathway without forming the fcc-phase."];
  "N33" [label="(19,0,0) Knowledge: This improved description of phase energetics captures the key role of the intermediate γ-phase in mediating the radiation response (contrasting Refs 23-26)."];
  "N37" [label="(16,0,0) Case: V2AlC and Nb2AlC follow common hex-γ-fcc pathway."];
  "N39" [label="(0,0,0) Common: Phase transformation behaviors in MAX phases follow compositional trends governed by atomic radius and electronegativity."];
  "N4" [label="(3,0,0) Case: Study of irradiation-induced polymorphism has been used to elucidate transformation mechanisms in ionic-covalent and metallic materials."];
  "N5" [label="(0,0,0) Common: Elucidating transformation mechanisms via irradiation polymorphism applies broadly across material classes."];
  "N7" [label="(4,0,0) Case: Many materials undergo multi-stage phase transformations under irradiation (e.g., pyrochlore/spinel oxides to defect-fluorite then amorphous)."];
  "N8" [label="(0,0,0) Common: Multi-stage phase transformations under irradiation are a recurring pattern in complex oxides and related ceramics."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes to explain and predict irradiation-induced phase behavior in MAX phases by combining in-situ ion-irradiation TEM with ab-initio calculations of the intermediate γ-phase, showing that V2AlC and Nb2AlC follow a hexagonal-to-γ-to-fcc pathway whereas Cr2AlC follows a hexagonal-to-γ-to-amorphous pathway, and relating these extreme-environment transformations to composition-dependent lattice and electronic factors governed by atomic radius and electronegativity.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Combining direct in-situ ion-irradiation TEM observations with ab-initio γ-phase energetics can connect observed multi-stage irradiation pathways to composition-dependent lattice and electronic factors."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) For the studied Cr2AlC, V2AlC, and Nb2AlC MAX phases, the contrast between hexagonal-phase to γ-phase to fcc transformation and Cr2AlC hexagonal-phase to γ-phase to amorphous transformation supports a proposed composition-dependent predictive rule based on atomic radius and electronegativity."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because mechanisms are difficult to understand by analyzing only initial phases, prior analyses that rarely studied intermediate phases leave the intermediate γ-phase role under-resolved."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) An antisite-defect criterion restricted to early stages before γ-phase formation cannot fully explain phase behavior mediated by the intermediate γ-phase."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Mostly ex situ irradiation experiments and mainly phenomenological initial-phase analyses left prior work without direct real-time observation of the evolving multi-stage pathway."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) The anomalous Cr2AlC hexagonal-phase to γ-phase to amorphous pathway can be explained by ab-initio calculations that include the intermediate γ-phase energetics."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Study of irradiation-induced polymorphism is used to elucidate transformation mechanisms across ionic-covalent and metallic materials."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Complex oxides and related ceramics provide examples in which irradiation can drive multi-stage transformations such as defect-fluorite formation followed by amorphization."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Directly observed MAX-phase irradiation behavior includes multi-stage transformation, with reported hexagonal-phase to intermediate γ-phase to fcc-phase behavior in the relevant MAX-phase cases."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because MAX phases are proposed for nuclear applications and can undergo irradiation-driven multi-stage transformations, they are important candidates for irradiation studies in extreme environments."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The observed hexagonal-phase to γ-phase to fcc-phase pathway shows that analyses based only on the initial hexagonal phase are insufficient to capture the role of the intermediate γ-phase."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The combination of initial-phase-limited analyses and the absence of substantial theoretical investigation of compositional trends creates a need for comprehensive understanding of multi-stage phase transformation behavior in MAX phases."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Investigating three representative M2AlC MAX phases under in-situ ion-irradiation TEM addresses the need for direct, comprehensive understanding of their multi-stage irradiation-induced transformation behavior."];
